NAME
    cov - record executed instruction addresses and discovered routines

SYNOPSIS
    cov on|off
    cov clear
    cov <addr> [<len>]
    cov mark
    cov new <addr> [<len>]
    cov funcs [<count>]
    cov save|load <file>
    cov <cpu> on|off|clear|<addr> [<len>]

DESCRIPTION
    Records every executed instruction address in a 2 MiB per-processor
    bitmap while armed, reporting whether code in any address range
    actually ran and identifying real opcode boundaries.

    Prefixing a processor name from cpus(1) (or 'cop' for the cartridge
    coprocessor) records or queries that processor's own instruction
    stream and address space independently.

OPTIONS
    on | off
        Start or stop recording executed addresses.

    clear
        Clear the recorded bitmap, differential mark, and discovered
        routine table.

    <addr> [<len>]
        Report which addresses within <len> hex bytes starting at <addr>
        (default 16) have executed.

    mark
        Snapshot the current coverage bitmap as a differential baseline
        (allocates a second 2 MiB bitmap) without stopping recording.

    new <addr> [<len>]
        Report only addresses in the range that executed since the last
        'cov mark'.

    funcs [<count>]
        List entry points actually reached via calls or interrupt vectors
        while recording, ordered busiest first, with call counts and entry
        kind. Output aligns with label(1) for naming discovered routines.

    save <file> | load <file>
        Write the coverage bitmap and routine table to
        /Logs/<CoreName>/<file>, or merge a saved map back into current
        coverage (summing routine entry counts). Paths starting with '/'
        resolve from the shell sandbox root; paths escaping the sandbox
        are refused (see dump(1) and hier(7)). Maps are not keyed to a
        specific ROM, so match filenames to the ROM (conventionally using
        a .covmap extension).

EXAMPLES
    cov on
    cov 10F452 10
    cov mark
    cov new 108000 8000
    cov funcs 20
    cov save alttp.covmap
    cov gsu 0A80E9 40
    cov spc on

EXIT STATUS
    0   Command completed (or usage message printed).
    1   Path outside sandbox, no target attached, unsupported processor,
        invalid argument, or I/O/session fault.

SEE ALSO
    cpus(1), bp(1), callers(1), disasm(1), label(1), profile(1),
    vectors(1), dump(1), hier(7)
